#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hash

#% text
# `hash(e)` gives a 64-bit hash of the term `e`.

#% text
# `hash(e, method)` gives the digest of `e` by `method`; `hash(e, method, format)` in the given format.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Methods: `"MD5"`, `"SHA"` (SHA-1), `"SHA224"`, `"SHA256"`, `"SHA384"`, `"SHA512"`, `"CRC32"`, `"Adler32"`, also written `["SHA", 256]`; `"Expression"` is the default 64-bit hash.

#% item
# A string is hashed as its UTF-8 bytes; another term as the UTF-8 bytes of `to_string(e)`, so equal terms have equal digests.

#% item
# Formats: `"Integer"` (the default, the digest as an unsigned integer), `"HexString"`, `"DecimalString"`, `"ByteList"` (the digest's bytes).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
hash("The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog", "SHA256", "HexString")
#% output
#= "d7a8fbb307d7809469ca9abcb0082e4f8d5651e46d3cdb762d02d0bf37c9e592"

#% cell
hash("abc", "MD5")
#% output
#= 191415658344158766168031473277922803570

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
hash("abc", "CRC32", "HexString")
#% output
#= "352441c2"

#% cell
hash("abc", "MD5", "ByteList")
#% output
#= [144, 1, 80, 152, 60, 210, 79, 176, 214, 150, 63, 125, 40, 225, 127, 114]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Equal terms have equal hashes:

#% cell
hash(x^2 + 1) === hash(1 + x^2)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `to_string`, `to_character_code`
